# PR #8 follow-up audit: MCP endpoint and OAuth 2.1 server

Nothing Critical, High or Medium. The live probe found no way into household data or onto any new path without a household member's consent. I fixed three issues, one commit each:
- **Tests:** full suite 133 passed; `mcp.integration` + `proxy-matcher` 16 passed, re-run by the challenger.
- **Static checks:** typecheck clean (exit 0); eslint ran on `src` and `tests` with no output shown.
- **Patches:** each applies on its own to `origin/main` (8dddae0). Your local `main` is stale at 91aedf4.

## Findings

**Low (highest of the Lows): trip text can leak booking codes.**
- `get_trip` returns free-text `title`, `location` and `notes` (`src/mcp/tools.ts:282-297`).
- The trip scrub only removes runs of 6 or more digits, so letter-and-number booking codes in notes (airline PNRs like `K7XQ2M`) reach the assistant.
- That contradicts `docs/SECURITY.md:99`, which says confirmation numbers are never included.
- **Fix (not done):** drop `notes` from MCP output, or scrub booking-code patterns from it. Or correct the docs.

**Low: refresh tokens worked indefinitely, and the refresh grant didn't check client_id. Fixed in `294947e`.**
- They are not rotated, and their 30-day expiry slid forward on every use (`server.ts:184-212`). `client_id` was optional.
- Now `client_id` is required and must match, and a grant ends 90 days after approval.
- Rotation with reuse detection is still worth adding if Gemini can handle it.

**Low: one attacker can block new assistant connections for about a day.**
- Registration has a global cap of 30 per day (`register/route.ts:16`) and a per-IP cap of 10 per hour, so three IPs fill it in about 3 hours. Existing connections are unaffected.
- The per-IP key trusts `x-real-ip` (`rate-limit.ts:33`). Railway staff say the edge overwrites it, but there is no official doc.
- **Fix (not done):** when the 50-client limit is hit, evict the oldest unapproved client instead of refusing everyone.

**Low: the code exchange skipped the redirect_uri check when it was omitted (`server.ts:159`). Fixed in `e9aad40`.**
- It is now required and must match exactly. PKCE already limited the risk.

**Low: discovery docs set Auth.js cookies on cacheable responses. Fixed in `50f9472`.**
- Evidence: `curl -sD- …/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server` returned `cache-control: public, max-age=3600` together with `set-cookie: __Host-authjs.csrf-token…` and `__Secure-authjs.callback-url…`.
- After the fix, on the local server: no cookies on `/.well-known/*`, security headers still sent, `/finance` still redirects to sign-in.

**Low: redirect URIs may use any path on an allowed host (`policy.ts:89`).**
- This only matters if an attacker can read a page on claude.ai or chatgpt.com, or the assistant's callback doesn't tie `state` to the session that started the flow (phishing with the attacker's own connector).
- **Fix (not done):** pin the known callback paths.

**Info:**
- Tool error messages under 200 characters go back to the assistant word for word, after scrubbing (`src/mcp/server.ts:71`).
- `client_name` can say "Claude", but the consent screen shows the real redirect host.
- `isPublicPath` matches `/signin` as a prefix (older code).
- `OPTIONS /api/mcp` sends no CORS headers (intended).
- npm audit: 4 moderate, all esbuild ≤0.24.2, a build tool, not runtime.
- `/.well-known/..%2ffinance` skips the proxy but still lands on 404 or the public discovery handlers. The test only checks the matcher pattern, not encoded paths.

**Checked and fine:**
- **PKCE and codes:** PKCE is S256 only and `plain` is rejected. Codes are single-use (atomic), live 5 minutes, and are bound to client, redirect URI and resource. Redeeming a code twice revokes what it produced.
- **Consent:** only an allowlisted member can approve (via `authedAction`). Next's server-action Origin check protects it against CSRF, and `frame-ancestors 'none'` stops framing.
- **Tokens and calls:** tokens are stored as SHA-256 hashes. The resource (audience) is checked, the allowlist is re-checked on every call, and browser Origins other than the app are refused on `/api/mcp`.
- **Tool output:** answers use whitelisted fields only; no account IDs, SimpleFIN URL or vault documents.
- **Errors:** error bodies are clean, with no stack traces.

## Live probe notes and things to watch
- **Test client:** I registered one, `hm_client_oJJtgjNRNFk0kJy1ngpS2LQ78HQerSiwJ8BGsx4irr4`, with redirect `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`. The server refuses `example.com`. Nobody approved it, so it is deleted automatically within 24 hours. No consent was completed and no tokens were obtained.
- **Possible break from fix 1:** if ChatGPT or Gemini refresh without sending `client_id` in the body, their refreshes will now fail. Watch the `[oauth]` logs after deploying.
- **Dev server command:** the required command fails because `next dev` doesn't accept `--strictPort` or `--host`. I used `--port 5300 --hostname 127.0.0.1` instead.

Sources on Railway's IP headers (community forum, not official): [which headers the edge sets](https://station.railway.com/questions/which-client-address-headers-does-the-ra-fb9e36d7), [X-Real-IP trust and spoofing](https://station.railway.com/questions/title-x-real-ip-header-trust-and-spoo-a8007be6)

